["<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>A goose is a flying engine wrapped in muscle, bone and feathers. Know where the engine sits and you know why steel BB, close range and the head matter.</p></aside><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-canada-goose-flight.jpg\" alt=\"A Canada goose in flight: a flying engine.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A Canada goose in flight: a flying engine. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Image taken by Alan D. Wilson, and modified by Diliff (cropped and noise reduction applied)., CC BY-SA 2.5, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canada_goose_flight_cropped_and_NR.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><ul><li>Big breast muscle on a deep keel: that is flight, and your meat <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Hollow bones join the air sacs <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: what this chapter covers</summary><ul><li>Skeleton and muscle, breathing with air sacs, heart and big vessels, the gut and gizzard, body heat, the fat cycle, lifespan.</li><li>Last screen: what it all means for the shot and the meat.</li><li>Bird body facts here come from general bird biology (Stanford Birds, ornithology lecture notes) plus goose studies. Where a fact is for birds in general, it says so.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Side cutaway of a goose. A big red breast muscle sits on the keel, the deep breastbone, and powers flight; it is the meat. The wing bones are hollow and the bones link to the air sacs, 80 percent. The legs are set far back.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Built to fly: keel, muscle, hollow bone</text><path d=\"M60 170 Q40 150 70 125 Q110 100 190 105 L250 100 Q262 60 285 40 Q300 30 318 38 L340 44 L318 50 Q300 50 292 70 Q282 100 290 130 Q300 175 250 195 Q180 215 110 205 Q75 198 60 170Z\" fill=\"#efe6d6\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M150 160 Q210 205 280 150 Q260 120 200 125 Q160 130 150 160Z\" fill=\"#c0392b\" opacity=\".75\"/><path d=\"M150 186 Q215 222 285 168\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"6\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M150 186 Q215 222 285 168\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M120 130 L170 118 L220 122\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"5\" fill=\"none\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><path d=\"M140 200 L130 250 L145 262 M165 205 L160 250 L178 262\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"4\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M262 92 Q275 60 295 44\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><text x=\"215\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Breast muscle</text><text x=\"215\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">= the meat</text><line x1=\"330\" y1=\"215\" x2=\"255.0\" y2=\"190.0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"255.0,190.0 262.9,189.0 260.7,195.6\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"232\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Keel</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"248\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">(breastbone)</text><line x1=\"90\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"140.0\" y2=\"122.0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"140.0,122.0 132.1,121.0 135.8,115.2\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"80\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Wing bones,</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"86\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">hollow</text><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"250\" x2=\"128.0\" y2=\"240.0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"128.0,240.0 121.5,244.7 120.3,237.8\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"60\" y=\"268\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Legs set</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"284\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">far back</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"280\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Bones link to air sacs (80%)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>The keel (breastbone) anchors the breast muscles <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Legs sit far back: a goose walks and swims well (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: muscle and bone</summary><ul><li>A bird's skeleton is light: some bones are hollow and linked to the air sacs; others are dense where strength matters (80%, Stanford Birds).</li><li>Air spaces in bones are more extensive in large birds than small ones (70%, search preview of a bird anatomy handout).</li><li>Flight power comes from the breast muscles on the keel. That is why a goose breast is so big <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Wing bones and the thick breast stand between a pellet from below and the vital organs (Tip, 65%).</li><li>Geese fly in V formation (80%, Audubon) and can travel more than 1,000 km in a day (80%, Hinterland Who's Who).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram anim\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 372\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Animation of a goose breathing, a Canada goose in side view with the body shown as a cutaway. The lungs sit high on the back, the front air sacs in the chest, the rear air sacs at the back of the body. Step 1, breath in: fresh air goes down the windpipe past the lungs into the rear air sacs. Step 2, breath out: the rear sacs push that air forward through the lungs, where oxygen is taken up. Step 3, the next breaths move the used air into the front sacs and out of the windpipe. Birds have 9 air sacs and fresh air crosses the lungs in both phases, 85 percent.\">\n<rect width=\"400\" height=\"372\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"22\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Air flows one way through the lungs</text>\n<path d=\"M44 150 L18 138 L22 160 L44 166z\" fill=\"#2a2622\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 200 L146 228 M200 202 L204 228 M136 230 h18 M196 230 h18\" stroke=\"#2a2622\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n<path d=\"M40 160 Q36 130 70 116 Q120 98 196 102 L254 98 Q276 100 288 112 Q304 136 290 170 Q270 206 210 210 Q150 214 100 206 Q56 196 40 160z\" fill=\"#efe6d6\" stroke=\"#5a4a3a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<path d=\"M262 108 Q272 76 284 54 Q292 38 312 36 Q330 34 338 46 L352 52 L338 58 Q326 60 316 58 Q302 62 298 80 Q292 100 290 118z\" fill=\"#24211e\"/>\n<path d=\"M312 46 Q318 58 326 58 Q320 50 318 42z\" fill=\"#f4efe4\"/>\n<circle cx=\"326\" cy=\"44\" r=\"2\" fill=\"#f4efe4\"/>\n<path d=\"M80 128 Q140 112 200 120 Q170 140 110 146 Q86 146 80 128z\" fill=\"#d8cdb8\" opacity=\".6\"/>\n<path d=\"M336 52 Q304 60 294 86 Q284 108 250 118\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#9a9ca2\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n<path d=\"M170 122 C186 108 226 106 244 116 C250 126 240 134 224 134 C200 136 182 134 170 128z\" fill=\"#e48d97\" stroke=\"#b5606b\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"252\" cy=\"158\" rx=\"24\" ry=\"20\" fill=\"#cfe0f0\" stroke=\"#7fa6c8\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"122\" cy=\"164\" rx=\"44\" ry=\"28\" fill=\"#cfe0f0\" stroke=\"#7fa6c8\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/>\n<line x1=\"196\" y1=\"116\" x2=\"166\" y2=\"81\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><circle cx=\"196\" cy=\"116\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><rect x=\"132.1\" y=\"52.0\" width=\"55.8\" height=\"29.0\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#6b5b4b\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"72.5\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">lungs</text>\n<line x1=\"292\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"307.466\" y2=\"108\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><circle cx=\"292\" cy=\"92\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><rect x=\"307.5\" y=\"96.0\" width=\"84.5\" height=\"29.0\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#6b5b4b\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n<text x=\"349.733\" y=\"116.5\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">windpipe</text>\n<line x1=\"258\" y1=\"172\" x2=\"280\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><circle cx=\"258\" cy=\"172\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><rect x=\"242.9\" y=\"240.0\" width=\"94.1\" height=\"29.0\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#6b5b4b\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n<text x=\"290\" y=\"260.5\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">front sacs</text>\n<line x1=\"116\" y1=\"184\" x2=\"104\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><circle cx=\"116\" cy=\"184\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><rect x=\"52.0\" y=\"240.0\" width=\"88.0\" height=\"29.0\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#6b5b4b\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n<text x=\"96\" y=\"260.5\" font-size=\"16\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">rear sacs</text>\n<path id=\"mgs-air-sacs-p1\" d=\"M334 52 Q300 60 290 86 Q280 108 244 120 Q186 132 124 164\" fill=\"none\"/>\n<path id=\"mgs-air-sacs-p2\" d=\"M124 164 Q144 130 172 122 L236 122 Q250 138 252 156\" fill=\"none\"/>\n<path id=\"mgs-air-sacs-p3\" d=\"M252 156 Q262 124 284 104 Q294 74 334 52\" fill=\"none\"/>\n<circle r=\"7\" fill=\"#2f6fb0\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"0s\"/><animateMotion dur=\"3s\" begin=\"0s\" fill=\"freeze\"><mpath href=\"#mgs-air-sacs-p1\"/></animateMotion></circle>\n<circle r=\"7\" fill=\"#e8590c\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"3s\"/><animateMotion dur=\"3s\" begin=\"3s\" fill=\"freeze\"><mpath href=\"#mgs-air-sacs-p2\"/></animateMotion></circle>\n<circle r=\"7\" fill=\"#7a7a7a\" stroke=\"#fffaf0\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"6s\"/><animateMotion dur=\"3s\" begin=\"6s\" fill=\"freeze\"><mpath href=\"#mgs-air-sacs-p3\"/></animateMotion></circle>\n<g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"0s\"/><text x=\"12\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f6fb0\">1. Breath in: fresh air to the rear sacs</text></g>\n<g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"3s\"/><text x=\"12\" y=\"312\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#c2410c\">2. Breath out: rear sacs push it through the lungs</text></g>\n<g opacity=\"0\"><set attributeName=\"opacity\" to=\"1\" begin=\"6s\"/><text x=\"12\" y=\"334\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#4d4d4d\">3. Next breaths: front sacs, then out</text></g>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"362\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">9 air sacs, fresh air in both phases (85%)</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption>Fresh air crosses the lungs on the way in and the way out. <button class=\"anim-replay\">↻ Replay</button></figcaption></figure><ul><li>Birds have 9 air sacs and one way air flow <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>The breathing system fills about one fifth of the body <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: how a goose breathes</summary><ul><li>The 9 air sacs: one interclavicular, two cervical, two front thoracic, two rear thoracic, two abdominal (85%, Weber State ornithology lecture).</li><li>The sacs do not trade oxygen. They are bellows that push air one way through stiff lungs <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Breath in: most fresh air goes to the rear sacs. Breath out: the rear sacs push it through the lungs. Used air moves to the front sacs and leaves on the next breath out <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>About one fifth of a bird's body volume is breathing system, versus about one twentieth in a mammal (80%, Stanford Birds).</li><li>A bird takes about one breath every 6 to 10 heartbeats; a mammal about one every 4.5 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Air sacs also help shed heat in hard flight <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>What it means for you: a pellet through the body cavity can collapse sacs, but a goose hit only in the sacs can still fly far (Tip, 60%).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Side cutaway of a goose. The lungs lie tight against the back ribs. The heart sits low and forward, at the base of the neck, with big vessels running up the neck. The liver lies behind the heart, and the gut and gizzard fill the rear of the body. Heart and lungs are in the front half of the body, 80 percent.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Where the engine sits</text><path d=\"M60 170 Q40 150 70 125 Q110 100 190 105 L250 100 Q262 60 285 40 Q300 30 318 38 L340 44 L318 50 Q300 50 292 70 Q282 100 290 130 Q300 175 250 195 Q180 215 110 205 Q75 198 60 170Z\" fill=\"#efe6d6\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><ellipse cx=\"205\" cy=\"120\" rx=\"34\" ry=\"12\" fill=\"#e57373\"/><path d=\"M238 145 Q252 130 262 148 Q260 170 244 178 Q230 165 238 145Z\" fill=\"#8e1b1b\"/><path d=\"M250 140 Q270 110 285 80\" stroke=\"#8e1b1b\" stroke-width=\"5\" fill=\"none\"/><ellipse cx=\"205\" cy=\"160\" rx=\"26\" ry=\"14\" fill=\"#7b4a2a\"/><ellipse cx=\"135\" cy=\"160\" rx=\"38\" ry=\"20\" fill=\"#cdb38a\"/><line x1=\"190\" y1=\"62\" x2=\"205.0\" y2=\"106.0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"205.0,106.0 199.4,100.3 206.0,98.1\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"180\" y=\"52\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Lungs: tight to the back ribs</text><line x1=\"335\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"262.0\" y2=\"160.0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"262.0,160.0 269.6,157.5 268.7,164.4\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"345\" y=\"190\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Heart: low,</text><text x=\"345\" y=\"206\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">front, under</text><text x=\"345\" y=\"222\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">the neck base</text><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"240\" x2=\"205.0\" y2=\"176.0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"205.0,176.0 207.9,183.5 201.0,182.9\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"256\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Liver</text><line x1=\"90\" y1=\"230\" x2=\"125.0\" y2=\"180.0\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"125.0,180.0 123.7,187.9 118.0,183.9\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"80\" y=\"246\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Gut and gizzard</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"262\" width=\"360\" height=\"32\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"283\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Heart and lungs: the front half of the body (80%)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Heart: low and forward, under the base of the neck <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Lungs: tight against the back ribs <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: heart and blood</summary><ul><li>Birds have relatively large four chamber hearts (80%, University of Connecticut lecture notes; Stanford Birds).</li><li>Resting heart rate in birds: about 150 to 350 beats a minute, higher in flight <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Blood pressure can reach 300 to 400 mm of mercury <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span>.</li><li>Big vessels run from the heart up the neck to the head. A neck hit cuts them (Tip, 65%).</li><li>Heart and lungs sit in the front half of the body, shielded by the keel and breast below and the back ribs above <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A goose gut drawn as a tube: bill, gullet, stomach, gizzard with grit that grinds grass, gut, vent. Food passes in about 1.5 hours in brent geese, 70 percent, and grazing geese digest only 27 to 34 percent of grass, 70 percent, so they eat most of the day.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">From bill to dropping in about 1.5 hours</text><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"365\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#cdb38a\" stroke-width=\"14\"/><circle cx=\"30\" cy=\"120\" r=\"18\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"30\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Bill</text><circle cx=\"90\" cy=\"120\" r=\"18\" fill=\"#a1887f\"/><text x=\"90\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Gullet</text><circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"120\" r=\"18\" fill=\"#c97b63\"/><text x=\"160\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Stomach</text><circle cx=\"230\" cy=\"120\" r=\"26\" fill=\"#6b4f35\"/><text x=\"230\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Gizzard</text><circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"120\" r=\"18\" fill=\"#cdb38a\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Gut</text><circle cx=\"365\" cy=\"120\" r=\"18\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/><text x=\"365\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Vent</text><g fill=\"#fff\"><circle cx=\"222\" cy=\"116\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#fff\"/><circle cx=\"228\" cy=\"122\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#fff\"/><circle cx=\"234\" cy=\"116\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#fff\"/><circle cx=\"240\" cy=\"122\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#fff\"/></g><text x=\"230\" y=\"190\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">grit grinds grass</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"206\" width=\"170\" height=\"84\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"105.0\" y=\"226\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Grass passes fast:</text><text x=\"105.0\" y=\"245\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">about 1.5 hours</text><text x=\"105.0\" y=\"264\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">(70%, brent geese)</text><rect x=\"210\" y=\"206\" width=\"170\" height=\"84\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"295.0\" y=\"226\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Digests only</text><text x=\"295.0\" y=\"245\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">27 to 34% of grass</text><text x=\"295.0\" y=\"264\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">(70%) so: eat all day</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Grit in the gizzard grinds grass <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Grass passes in about 1.5 hours <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>. They eat most of the day.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: digestion in full</summary><ul><li>Path: bill, gullet, glandular stomach (proventriculus), muscular gizzard, intestine with two blind pouches (ceca), vent (85%, FAO poultry digestion chapter).</li><li>The gizzard is the grinding mill. It needs grit, small stones the goose swallows <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>A true crop in geese: sources disagree. <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>. Hunters often find grain packed in the gullet (Tip).</li><li>Brent geese passed food in about 1.5 hours (70%, Wildfowl journal). Grazing upland geese digest only 27 to 34% of grass organic matter <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>One barnacle goose study estimated about 160 droppings a day (60%, crude estimate). Lots of droppings is good sign for you. See <a href=\"#/s/mc-geese-08\">Chapter 8</a>.</li><li>Geese often feed 12 hours a day or more (80%, Hinterland Who's Who).</li><li>Canada geese on rougher, high fibre food ate less, held it longer and grew bigger guts <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Three thermometers. You: 37 degrees Celsius. A resting bird: about 38.5 degrees. An active bird: about 41 degrees, 85 percent. Down feathers trap heat and waterfowl feet feel less cold.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Body heat: a goose runs hot</text><rect x=\"50\" y=\"60\" width=\"20\" height=\"180\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"54\" y=\"128\" width=\"12\" height=\"112\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#e8a87c\"/><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"248\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#e8a87c\"/><text x=\"60\" y=\"276\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">You</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"294\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">37°C</text><text x=\"76\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">37°C</text><rect x=\"140\" y=\"60\" width=\"20\" height=\"180\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"144\" y=\"104.0\" width=\"12\" height=\"136.0\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"248\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#e8590c\"/><text x=\"150\" y=\"276\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Resting</text><text x=\"150\" y=\"294\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">goose (85%)</text><text x=\"166\" y=\"114.0\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">38.5°C</text><rect x=\"235\" y=\"60\" width=\"20\" height=\"180\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"239\" y=\"64\" width=\"12\" height=\"176\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><circle cx=\"245\" cy=\"248\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><text x=\"245\" y=\"276\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Active</text><text x=\"245\" y=\"294\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">goose (85%)</text><text x=\"261\" y=\"74\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">41°C</text><rect x=\"310\" y=\"60\" width=\"85\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"352.5\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Down</text><text x=\"352.5\" y=\"99\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">traps</text><text x=\"352.5\" y=\"118\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">heat</text><rect x=\"310\" y=\"178\" width=\"85\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"352.5\" y=\"198\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Feet feel</text><text x=\"352.5\" y=\"216\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">less cold</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Birds run warmer than you: about 38.5°C resting, 41°C active <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Down traps heat; feet feel less cold <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: cold and heat</summary><ul><li>Mean bird body temperature: 38.5°C resting, 41.0°C active, 43.9°C in hard activity (85%, Prinzinger and others 1991, search preview).</li><li>Waterfowl feet have fewer nerves, so they stand on ice and snow for long spells (80%, Ducks Unlimited senses article).</li><li>Cold changes behaviour: below about minus 9°C (15°F) at sunrise, a big wintering flock usually skipped its morning flight (80%, Raveling and others 1972). See <a href=\"#/s/mc-geese-06\">Chapter 6</a>.</li><li>A shot goose holds heat in its feathers. Cool it fast (Tip). See <a href=\"#/s/mc-geese-10\">Chapter 10</a>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Line chart of body reserves across the year, a Tip, shape only. Reserves peak in early spring before nesting, drop while the hen sits on the eggs, stay low through the summer moult, then climb in fall on grain. The hen stores enough for about a month of incubation, 80 percent.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Reserves through the year (Tip)</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">J</text><text x=\"69\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">F</text><text x=\"98\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">M</text><text x=\"127\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">A</text><text x=\"156\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">M</text><text x=\"185\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">J</text><text x=\"214\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">J</text><text x=\"243\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">A</text><text x=\"272\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">S</text><text x=\"301\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">O</text><text x=\"330\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">N</text><text x=\"359\" y=\"262\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">D</text><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"245\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"245\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M40 150 Q70 120 98 100 Q120 95 140 170 Q160 215 185 205 Q215 190 240 185 Q270 170 300 140 Q330 125 360 130\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"4\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"95\" y=\"88\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Peak before nesting</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"232\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Hen burns it sitting</text><text x=\"225\" y=\"175\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Moult costs</text><text x=\"320\" y=\"118\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Fall: fatten on grain</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"288\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Hen stores enough for about a month of sitting (80%)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Hens store reserves for about a month of sitting <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Fall: they fatten on grain (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the year in reserves</summary><ul><li>Before nesting, geese feed hard to store reserves that carry the hen through about one month of incubation (80%, Hinterland Who's Who).</li><li>The hen sits 25 to 28 days and eats little <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. She is at her leanest after hatch (Tip).</li><li>Moult costs: a study of Illinois geese found the largest nutrition costs of moult from July to November <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li>The chart shape is a Tip, built from these facts, not a measured curve.</li><li>For the table: fall birds on grain are often fatter than early September birds (Tip).</li></ul></details>","<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Age</th><th>What it means (80%, Hinterland Who's Who)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Gosling</td><td>Flies at 6 to 9 weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Year 1</td><td>Stays with parents all year</td></tr><tr><td>Year 2</td><td>Finds a mate</td></tr><tr><td>Year 2 or 3</td><td>First nest</td></tr><tr><td>10 to 24 years</td><td>Old hand of the flock</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>Most that survive year one live 10 years or more, up to about 24 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>About half of goslings survive to fly <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: longevity records</summary><ul><li>A Canada goose banded at the Jack Miner sanctuary in Ontario was at least 23 years old when found (70%, Auk 1955, search preview).</li><li>Older sources list longer records; the current official banding record could not be read here. <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>.</li><li>Old birds have seen many hunters. They are often the wary ones (Tip). See <a href=\"#/s/mc-geese-04\">Chapter 4</a>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 300\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Side view of a goose with three zones. Head and neck, shaded orange: the aim point on close decoying birds, a Tip. Heart and lungs in the front of the body, 80 percent. The rear gut area grey: hits there make cripples. Use steel BB, birds feet down, inside 35 metres, 38 yards, a Tip.\">\n  <rect width=\"400\" height=\"300\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Where a pellet kills a goose</text><g transform=\"translate(-10 10)\"><path d=\"M60 170 Q40 150 70 125 Q110 100 190 105 L250 100 Q262 60 285 40 Q300 30 318 38 L340 44 L318 50 Q300 50 292 70 Q282 100 290 130 Q300 175 250 195 Q180 215 110 205 Q75 198 60 170Z\" fill=\"#efe6d6\" stroke=\"#2f3a2b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></g><path d=\"M250 112 Q262 70 285 50 Q300 40 318 48 L340 54 L318 60 Q300 60 292 80 Q282 110 290 140\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"18\" opacity=\".35\"/><ellipse cx=\"225\" cy=\"148\" rx=\"45\" ry=\"32\" fill=\"#e8590c\" opacity=\".45\"/><ellipse cx=\"120\" cy=\"170\" rx=\"55\" ry=\"30\" fill=\"#9e9e9e\" opacity=\".35\"/><text x=\"330\" y=\"96\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Head and neck</text><text x=\"330\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">(Tip: aim here)</text><text x=\"225\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Heart, lungs</text><text x=\"225\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">(80%)</text><text x=\"115\" y=\"172\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Gut: cripples</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"232\" width=\"360\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"254\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Steel BB, feet down, inside 35 m (38 yd)</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"276\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">(Tip, from the quick lesson)</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Head, neck, front body: clean kills (Tip, 65%).</li><li>Rear body hits: cripples that fly on (Tip).</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: shot and meat notes</summary><ul><li>Thick feathers, big muscle and strong wing bones stop small pellets. Use steel BB for big Canada geese (70%, quick lesson; Ducks Unlimited).</li><li>Pattern: at least 60 pellets in a 76 cm (30 in) circle at your longest range (65%, Ducks Unlimited).</li><li>On close decoying birds aim at the head, which is about the size of a dove's body (65%, Ducks Unlimited). Fewer pellets end up in the breast <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li>Max range for you this season: 35 m (38 yd) (Tip, from the quick lesson).</li><li>Meat: the breast is the prize, about 1 kg boned from a big Canada goose <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">60%</span>. Legs are worth slow cooking (Tip).</li><li>Full detail: <a href=\"#/s/mc-geese-10\">Chapter 10</a> and <a href=\"#/s/shot-placement\">Shot placement</a>.</li></ul></details>","<aside class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Memory rhymes (made for this app)</div><ul><li>\"Keel and breast, the engine's chest.\"</li><li>\"Nine sacs breathe one way: air in, air through, air away.\"</li><li>\"Heart up front and low: aim the head, and down they go.\"</li></ul></aside><ul><li>Number trick: <strong>9, 41, 1.5</strong>. 9 air sacs, about 41°C active, about 1.5 hours from bill to dropping.</li></ul><aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Old hand's rule (tradition, opinion)</div><p>Shoot the head, not the goose. Widely taught by goose guides: aim at the white cheek, not the whole bird.</p></aside>","<ul><li>Stanford Birds, ornithology lecture notes, Hinterland Who's Who, Ducks Unlimited, Wildfowl journal studies. Checked 2026-10-11.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: every source and date checked</summary><ul><li><a href=\"https://web.stanford.edu/group/stanfordbirds/text/essays/Adaptations.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Stanford Birds essay, Adaptations</a>, read directly 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://faculty.weber.edu/jcavitt/OrnLect/Lecture%2010.%20Avian%20Respiration.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Weber State University, avian respiration lecture</a>, search preview 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://rubegalab.uconn.edu/2016/03/07/lecture-notes-respiration-and-circulation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">University of Connecticut, Rubega lab, respiration and circulation</a>, read directly 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.gwern.net/doc/psychology/animal/bird/1991-prinzinger.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Prinzinger and others 1991, Body temperature in birds</a>, search preview 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.fao.org/3/y4359e/y4359e05.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">FAO, digestive physiology of poultry</a> and a Wildfowl journal note on domestic goose anatomy, search previews 2026-10-11 (gizzard 85%, crop <span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span>).</li><li><a href=\"https://wildfowl.wwt.org.uk/index.php/wildfowl/article/view/927\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wildfowl journal, food throughput time in grazing waterfowl</a>, search preview 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">70%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.hww.ca/en/wildlife/birds/canada-goose.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hinterland Who's Who, Canada goose</a>, read directly 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.ducks.org/hunting/goose-hunting-tips-tactics/the-ultimate-guide-to-hunting-canada-geese\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ducks Unlimited, The ultimate guide to hunting Canada geese</a>, read directly 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">65%</span>.</li><li><a href=\"https://archive.org/download/biostor-200086/biostor-200086.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Raveling, Crews and Klimstra 1972, Wilson Bulletin 84:278</a>, read directly 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Full log: <code>research/2026-10-11-masterclass-geese.md</code>.</li></ul></details>"]